3. What does relText, shareText and atpeak with html?
selected is one number: the kernel at the slider divided by the kernel at 
p=0.45. At the peak it is 1. Anywhere else it is between 0 and 1. 
relText and shareText are two ways of writing that same number.

relText is the relative likelihood itself. If it is at least 0.001, 
it is shown with three decimals, such as 0.500. If it is positive 
but smaller than that, the text is < 0.001. If it is exactly 0, the text is 0.

shareText is that same value as a percent of the peak, 
so share = selected * 100. At or above 1% it uses one decimal (50.0%). 
From 0.01% up to 1% it uses two decimals. Anything smaller but still 
positive becomes < 0.01%, and an exact zero becomes 0%.

atPeak is true when the slider is on 0.45. That picks which sentence to show. 
On the peak, the sentence stops after the relative likelihood and says this value has the greatest support. Off the peak, it adds the percent sentence, using shareText.

html is an Observable function that turns a template string into a real HTML element. 
The backticks are the template, and ${...} drops a JavaScript value into it. <em> italicizes p, 
and <strong> bolds the number. class="binomial-readout" is only a CSS hook for styling.

The return builds one wrapper div. Inside it, in order: the y-axis label, 
the plot SVG, the sentence chosen above, and the legend. ${plot}
and ${readout} insert those already-built elements into that HTML.